NAME
       xxd - show a file as hexadecimal numbers

SYNOPSIS
       xxd [FILE]

DESCRIPTION
       xxd prints a hex dump of FILE (or standard input): each line starts
       with the offset, then 16 bytes as hexadecimal pairs grouped two bytes
       at a time, then the same bytes as text, with a dot for each byte that
       is not printable. It shows what really is in a file, such as Windows
       line endings (0d 0a) or a missing final newline. It comes with vim.

EXAMPLES
           printf 'hi\r\n' | xxd

SEE ALSO
       od(1), hexdump(1), strings(1)
